shairport-sync-desktop (5.6.2-3) trixie; urgency=medium

  * Install the desktop defaults as /etc/shairport-sync-desktop.conf and
    make each user's settings from that. /etc/shairport-sync.conf is the
    distribution's shairport-sync conffile: replacing that package after its
    file was edited stopped at a conffile prompt, failed when run without a
    terminal, and by default kept the old file, which put the receiver's
    D-Bus and MPRIS interfaces on the system bus with ALSA output. On
    upgrade, the old file and the stock sample are removed, or kept as
    .dpkg-bak if they were edited.
  * Start shairport-sync with --dbus-default-message-bus=session, so the
    D-Bus and MPRIS interfaces are on the session bus even when a user's
    settings name no bus.
  * Never run the receiver or NQPTP for system accounts, such as the login
    screen's, which advertised a receiver of their own at the login screen.
    Only one user at a time can run the receiver, as its ports belong to the
    whole machine; a second user's copy now stops retrying after a few
    tries. shairport-sync no longer starts when NQPTP cannot be started,
    instead of quietly falling back to classic AirPlay. Restarting NQPTP,
    which systemd also does after a crash, now restarts shairport-sync with
    it, ending a session in progress.
  * Keep each user's cover art in a private directory under
    $XDG_RUNTIME_DIR instead of /tmp/shairport-sync, which all users shared
    and another local user could point elsewhere, for example at a
    directory the receiver would then empty. Settings copied by earlier
    versions are moved over too, unless cover_art_cache_directory was set
    in them by hand.
  * Add a firewalld service, airplay-receiver, that opens the receiver's
    ports.
  * Depend on dbus-user-session, which provides the session bus the
    receiver's systemd user service publishes on.
  * Reload each user's systemd manager before restarting the receiver on
    upgrade, so changed unit files take effect, and start the receiver
    again in open sessions after a reinstall that follows "apt remove",
    unless the admin disabled it. Users whose systemd manager has stopped
    are left alone instead of getting a new one started.
  * Enable the receiver when the package replaces Ubuntu's shairport-sync,
    whose disabled user unit of the same name kept it off, and on the
    upgrade of a 5.6.2-2 that was left off that way, starting it in open
    sessions too. A receiver the admin disabled stays off.
  * Add a manual page for shairport-sync-gui, and ship the app's README as
    README.gui.md, with upstream's README.md, as the RPM does.
  * AirPlay Receiver: never restart the receiver while a sender is
    connected, paused senders included, or start one that was stopped. A
    restart still due to apply a setting is remembered when the app is
    closed.
  * AirPlay Receiver: read and change the output and volume settings with a
    parser for libconfig's syntax, so settings edited by hand (":"
    separators, a missing ";", several settings on a line, one-line groups,
    /* */ comments, an active line under the commented-out example) no
    longer end up with duplicate settings that stop the receiver, or with
    changes the receiver ignores. Saving is atomic, keeps the file's
    permissions, owner and symlinks, and refuses a read-only file or one
    with a syntax error, naming the line. Bytes that aren't UTF-8 no longer
    stop the app from starting, output names containing "${" are saved so
    shairport-sync takes them literally, and output_backend and mixer_type
    are matched ignoring case, as shairport-sync does.
  * AirPlay Receiver: pick up output and volume settings changed in the
    file by hand, and a receiver that was still starting when the app
    first looked. Stepping through outputs with the wheel or arrow keys
    saves and restarts once, and the picker always shows the real choice,
    also after a failed save.
  * AirPlay Receiver: with Use Device Volume, keep AirPlay on the device
    whose volume the sender controls until the session ends, and warn, then
    restart the receiver, when that device wasn't there when it started.
    Only the receiver's own PipeWire stream is moved, and only with the
    PipeWire backend.
  * AirPlay Receiver: show a receiver or NQPTP that keeps failing, with a
    Stop action, and let Start and Restart clear systemd's start limit.
  * AirPlay Receiver: one window per user; launching it again brings that
    window forward. A window that can't load is reported in a dialog,
    Plasma's Qt Quick style is used only where it is installed, and the
    footer shows whether AirPlay 2 or classic AirPlay is in use. The volume
    slider's arrow keys step from the current volume, and Space no longer
    blocks focused buttons.
  * AirPlay Receiver: copy a new user's settings from
    /etc/shairport-sync-desktop.conf, or from /etc/shairport-sync.conf
    where that is missing (installs from source).
  * gui/README.md: document the 5.6.2-3 packages, including Ubuntu 26.10,
    the container builds, the new defaults file, the firewall and one
    receiver per computer, and a from-source setup with the user units the
    app needs. gui/install.sh checks for the Qt Quick modules the window
    needs, says when the receiver isn't set up that way or the package's
    app is installed too, and removes the app again with --uninstall.
  * debian/copyright: add tinyhttp (BSD-2-Clause, linked in), pair_ap and
    the other third-party files, the GPL-2 NQPTP header, and the packaging/
    and rpm/ directories.
  * build-deb.sh: Ctrl-C now stops the build and removes the container and
    the temporary copy of the tree.
  * Ignore debian/autoreconf.before and debian/autoreconf.after in git.

 -- Ari-Santa <apeach@live.no>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 12:43:22 +0100

shairport-sync-desktop (5.6.2-2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * AirPlay Receiver: add "Use Device Volume" to the menu. When it is on,
    the sender's volume control sets the output device's own volume
    (pipewire.mixer_type = "sink") instead of turning the audio down in
    software, so the audio keeps its full resolution at any volume. Like an
    output change, it takes effect once no sender is connected.
  * Add debian/build-deb.sh, which builds the package in a podman container
    for a given release, e.g. "debian/build-deb.sh ubuntu:26.10".

 -- Ari-Santa <apeach@live.no>  Wed, 04 Nov 2026 21:12:24 +0100

shairport-sync-desktop (5.6.2-1) trixie; urgency=high

  * New upstream release 5.6.2:
    - Security fix: malformed DACP TLV tokens could cause out-of-bounds
      reads (GHSA-vfxr-hxq9-9h35).
    - 64-bit RTP timestamps on PTP connections, so AirPlay 2 sessions longer
      than about 12.5 hours no longer go silent.
  * Share the user units and the desktop default settings with the new RPM
    packaging (packaging/).

 -- Ari-Santa <apeach@live.no>  Tue, 03 Nov 2026 18:21:18 +0100

shairport-sync-desktop (5.6.1-2) trixie; urgency=medium

  * AirPlay Receiver: add an Output picker. "Follow System" keeps AirPlay
    audio on the system default output; any other choice pins it to that
    device. Playing streams move immediately through PipeWire metadata; the
    saved pipewire.sink_target is applied by restarting the receiver once
    no sender is connected.

